<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">I&nbsp;has&nbsp;a&nbsp;feedback.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">This&nbsp;is&nbsp;my&nbsp;feedback.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Although SCP-1970 requires minimal security, it must be isolated from other anomalous objects.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Shouldn't this be basic procedure for any object in containment?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When SCP-1970 was not in operation it was possible to hear movement within the casing, as well as occasional breathing noises, including snoring.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Are these little things really so loud that we can hear them breathing?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>However, due to his condition he was living alone and unable to convince any family members or caretakers that his television was &quot;filled with tiny, writhing men.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why not? Why couldn't he just show them?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Am I right to assume that the small organisms within the set were meant to originally act as a way to show images on a screen rather than using pixels? Anyways, I quite enjoyed it, but I really think the emotional impact is swift, fairly weak, and quickly forgotten about. +1 nonetheless</p>

							</div>

								
					
